This session made me look at product management a little differently.
Earlier, I used to think of a product lifecycle mainly as planning, building and launching.
I understood that it is actually a continuous learning process, where the product keeps changing based on feedback and evidence.

One thing I found interesting was the difference between a project and a product.
In a project, the customer and requirement may already be known, but a product has to work for many different users and situations.

The discussion about “good enough vs perfect” was also useful for me.
The credit-card example showed how the first version may not be perfect, but real usage can teach us things that planning alone cannot.

The discussion on roadmaps also made sense to me.
We can be more detailed about the near-term work, but longer-term plans should remain flexible because there is more uncertainty.


The AI Product Management part was particularly relevant to my work.
I understood that being an AI Product Manager is not simply about selecting an AI model.
The starting point should always be the problem, user and expected business outcome.

I can relate this to automation projects as well.
Before starting an automation or AI initiative, I should ask what problem we are solving, what evidence we have, and how we will know that the solution actually created value.

Overall, my biggest takeaway from this session is that product management is more about making good decisions under uncertainty than simply managing a project.
